{% if consanguinity_excluded %}
**Recurrent parental pregnancy loss is consistent with biallelic lethality.** Recurrent spontaneous abortion (HP:0200067) is among the released features. Runs of homozygosity give F_ROH(>=1.5 Mb) = {{ roh_f }}, so the parents are not detectably related, and each pregnancy of two unrelated carriers has a 25% chance of being biallelic. Complete knockout in mouse is embryonic-lethal with complete penetrance, between implantation and placentation. If biallelic loss is lethal in humans too, recurrent early pregnancy loss is the predicted consequence, and it is what the family history records. This is independent human evidence, from this child's own family, for the premise the whole null-plus-hypomorph argument rests on. It is consistent with that premise rather than proof of it: recurrent miscarriage is common and has many causes, and no products of conception were genotyped.
{% endif %}

**Residual function — conditional, and stated as such.** Simmons et al. (Hum Mol Genet 2019;28:1822, PMID 30668728) report that nearly complete loss of BubR1 is required for microcephaly. Microcephaly is not among the features released to participants. **If** it is genuinely absent rather than unreported or unassessed, that would imply the missense allele retains meaningful activity, and strategies acting on residual protein would be acting on a partially functional system. The antecedent is not established here and is not asserted: under the epistemic rule above, absence from the released set is not evidence of absence. Two further cautions apply — microcephaly is reported in 87% of MVA1 cases but is explicitly not required for the diagnosis (Callier P et al., Am J Med Genet A 2005;137:204-7, PMID 16059936), and this child is young, so a head-circumference trajectory may not be complete. The inference is recorded because it would matter if resolved, not because it is resolved.

### 2. Pharmacogenomics by clinical context

Results are grouped by the clinical contexts this child actually faces, not by gene, and ordered by how well each is supported by evidence about this child. A normal or negative genotype in a class this child will meet is reported as a finding in its own row. Every genotype interpretation below is PharmCAT's own CPIC, DPWG or FDA annotation for the called genotype; FDA text is shown with its source and is never counted as a guideline flag. Curated literature is shown separately from guidelines and labelled as such.

> **On the clinical features.** The eight HPO terms released to hackathon participants are the features *made available*, not the complete clinical picture. A feature's presence is evidence it is present; its **absence from that set is not evidence it is absent** — it may be absent, may not have been assessed, or may simply not have been released. Nothing below says this child lacks a feature, and any inference that depends on a feature being genuinely absent is written as conditional.

